From 8aa0d7d39849735c18673b10a9edc296ecf30f64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 21:32:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] nvk/rm: timeline syncs on RM semaphores

A vk_sync is a 64-bit value in a per-device pool of GPU-visible,
CPU-cached system memory (our own pages, 64 KiB chunks).  The physical
device exposes it as its timeline type, plus Mesa's vk_sync_binary on top
for binary semaphores and fences.

  - GPU signal: host SEM_EXECUTE release (64-bit payload, RELEASE_WFI)
    followed by NON_STALL_INTERRUPT, emitted in the context's push slots
    after the submit, then GP_PUT and the doorbell.
  - GPU wait: SEM_EXECUTE ACQ_STRICT_GEQ with ACQUIRE_SWITCH_TSG, emitted
    before the next push.  Values already reached are skipped.
  - CPU signal: store; CPU wait/get_value: read, spin briefly, then
    poll() the non-stall interrupt event (or sleep with backoff).
  - WAIT_PENDING: a per-sync "highest submitted signal" value.
  - Bind contexts wait and signal on the CPU.

WAIT_BEFORE_SIGNAL is not advertised, so the runtime runs in assisted
timeline mode and holds back submits that wait on unsubmitted values,
rather than leaving a GPU acquire spinning on a value nobody submitted.
Export/import (sync_file, opaque fd) is not supported yet: that needs an
RM semaphore surface bridged to DRM syncobjs through nvidia-drm.

